<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of San Carlos of Guatemala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as USAC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of San Carlos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> is the country's oldest and only public university, founded by royal decree in 1676 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros, today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigua-guatemala">Antigua Guatemala</a>, and later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. Granted autonomy by the revolutionary government in 1944–1945, it became a centre of political opposition, and many of its students and teachers were killed during the civil war.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The university grew out of the Colegio de Santo Tomás de Aquino, founded in 1562 by Bishop Francisco Marroquín. The royal decree of Carlos II establishing it is dated 31 January 1676, and Pope Innocent XI consecrated it on 18 June 1687, making it the third royal college founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> and the fourth university of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Colonial residents had demanded places in the royal institutions for their descendants, yet there were no schools to train public servants. From the 16th to the 19th centuries it taught civil and liturgical law, theology, philosophy, medicine and indigenous languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the earthquakes of 1773 destroyed much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de los Caballeros, the crown moved the university with the capital to La Nueva Guatemala de la Asunción. Independence took away its royal status. In 1829 the liberal general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> defeated the conservative regime of Mariano de Aycinena y Piñol and the university was suspended; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-galvez">Mariano Gálvez</a> founded a Science Academy in its place in 1834 that dropped religious teaching for mathematics, physics and engineering. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> restored the Pontifical University in 1840, with Juan José de Aycinena y Piñol again its head, and the Concordat of 1854 entrusted education to the regular clergy.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1871">Liberal Revolution of 1871</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> closed the Pontifical University in July 1875 and replaced it with colleges of law and of medicine and pharmacy forming the National University. Following the revolution of 20 October 1944, Decree 12 of 11 November 1944 granted autonomy and the present name, and women and others formerly excluded were admitted. Under President Miguel Ydígoras Fuentes the university suffered repression for siding with the labour unions, and three students were murdered in front of the College of Law in 1962.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The university has 40 academic units: 10 colleges, 10 schools, 19 regional centres and a technical institute. Each college is run by a dean elected for four years, with a board of a secretary and five trustees, two of them professors, one from the professional association and two students. It was the only university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> until 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Universidad de San Carlos de Guatemala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…president from 1945 to 1951, introduced a new constitution, social security, a labour code and autonomy for the <b>University of San Carlos</b>, and his successor Jacobo Árbenz enacted the agrarian reform of Decree 900 in 1952, which expropriated uncultivated land on large estates, including much of United Fruit’s…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
